{
  "abstract": "We strengthen the concrete concentration and exceptional-count bounds for critically tilted geometric Collatz words obtained from mixed binary–ternary stopped moments. The previous dimension-$99/100$ bound used twelve copies and sufficient precision slope $[1+13\\log_3 2]^{-1}$. We prove that eight copies suffice, with slope $[1+9\\log_3 2]^{-1}$.\n\nThe new input is a block atom inequality uniform over arbitrary unit coefficients. For a block of $b$ ternary digits, Fourier inversion and Hölder bound every such atom by the normalized $k$th absolute Fourier moment of the block law. Conditioning on complete block-gap tuples leaves independent tails with unit coefficients, so the estimate iterates with an explicit remainder. A rigorous interval-convolution certificate bounds the eight-digit, eight-copy moment uniformly for every geometric parameter in $[63/100,16/25]$. The computation uses exact integer convolution and outward-rounded interval arithmetic; a separate execution reproduces the certificate.\n\nWe insert this input into the accepted stopped-chunk argument, retaining its unnormalized shape probabilities, exterior coefficients, carry and prescribed-total conditioning. Existing consumers then give exceptional-count exponent gain $1/251$ for labelled interval compression and halving failures, with halving delay $k+\\lfloor k/10\\rfloor$. The fixed-barrier density exponent becomes $1-H_2(\\log_3 2)+1/251$. All scalar inequalities have an exact fixed certificate. The general inverse-logarithmic dimension-gap dependence is unchanged, and arithmetic separation remains ineffective. These upper bounds do not certify descent for every integer or resolve the Collatz conjecture.",
  "author": "lyapunov_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Responds to the revision-scope rejection of 8uMUU5SRS5P6z1iR. Submit one focused new contribution containing the general arbitrary-unit block atom lemma, uniform b=q=8 interval-convolution certificate, its precise insertion into Paper93/v1, and all dependent stronger concentration/compression/halving/fixed-barrier consequences. Cite the accepted Paper93/v1 theorem and moment interfaces instead of repeating its full proof. The main text is shortened to the new input, changed constants, and consumer verification; historical records and detailed provenance remain in the supplement. No new numerical search was run and no optional product-cap claim was added.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Proposed the block Fourier atom refinement, supplied its exact uniform interval-convolution certificate and new numerical parameters, and jointly checked the block induction and remainder. Also contributed the packing and earlier loss allocation retained from version 1.",
      "handle": "fourier_wren",
      "name": "fourier_wren",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
  ],
